Spring Security Annotations.


1. What is Spring Security?
Spring Security is the security framework commonly used with Spring
applications. It provides:
- Authentication: verifies who the caller is.
- Authorization: decides what an authenticated (or anonymous)
  caller is allowed to do.
- Protection against common attacks: including CSRF and
  session-fixation concerns, plus security headers and secure request
  handling.
- Integration: supports form login, HTTP Basic, OAuth 2.0, OpenID
  Connect, JWT-based resource servers, LDAP, and custom authentication
  mechanisms.
A typical request flow is:
1. A client sends an HTTP request.
2. The servlet filter chain processes it.
3. Authentication filters or a configured authentication mechanism
   establish the caller's identity.
4. The authenticated identity is stored in the SecurityContext.
5. Request-level authorization rules decide whether the URL can be
   accessed.
6. If the request reaches a secured service method, method-level
   authorization may run as well.
7. The controller/service executes only if the applicable checks pass.
Important: Authentication and authorization are different. A valid
JWT, for example, establishes an identity; it does not automatically
mean that the caller may access every endpoint.
2. The main annotations
2.1 @EnableWebSecurity
Purpose: Enables Spring Security's web-security integration and
allows the application to configure web security.

In a Spring Boot application, security auto-configuration already
provides much of the default setup. An explicit @EnableWebSecurity is
often not necessary when using Boot's standard configuration, but it may
be used on a configuration class when you want to make the web-security
setup explicit or customize it.

Example:
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/public/**").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers("/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());

        return http.build();
    }
}
Senior-level point: In modern Spring Security, the recommended
configuration style is to expose a SecurityFilterChain bean rather
than extend the old WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter. That adapter was
deprecated and removed from current Spring Security usage.
2.2 @EnableMethodSecurity
Purpose: Enables method-level authorization annotations such as
@PreAuthorize, @PostAuthorize, and (when enabled) @Secured and
JSR-250 annotations.

Example:
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity(
    securedEnabled = true,
    jsr250Enabled = true
)
public class MethodSecurityConfig {
}
By default, pre/post annotations are enabled. securedEnabled and
jsr250Enabled opt in to the corresponding annotation families.

Important distinction: @EnableWebSecurity configures web security;
@EnableMethodSecurity enables authorization checks on Spring-managed
methods. They solve related but different problems.
2.3 @PreAuthorize
Purpose: Evaluates an authorization expression before a method
executes. It is generally the most flexible and commonly recommended
method-security annotation.

Example --- role-based authorization:
@Service
public class UserService {

    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public List<User> getAllUsers() {
        return userRepository.findAll();
    }
}
Example --- permission or ownership check:
@PreAuthorize("hasAuthority('USER_READ')")
public User getUser(Long userId) {
    return userRepository.findById(userId)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(userId));
}
@PreAuthorize("#userId == authentication.principal.userId or hasRole('ADMIN')")
public User getUserProfile(Long userId) {
    return userRepository.findById(userId)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(userId));
}
Common expressions:
  Expression                                       Meaning
  isAuthenticated()                              Caller is authenticated

  hasRole('ADMIN')                               Caller has role ROLE_ADMIN by
default

  hasAnyRole('ADMIN', 'SUPPORT')                 Caller has at least one listed role

  hasAuthority('ORDER_READ')                     Caller has the exact authority

  hasAnyAuthority('ORDER_READ', 'ORDER_WRITE')   Caller has at least one listed
authority

  principal                                      Current authenticated principal

  authentication                                 Current Authentication object
#parameterName                                 A method argument, when parameter
names are available/configured
hasRole vs hasAuthority:
- hasRole("ADMIN") applies the conventional ROLE_ prefix, so it
  checks for ROLE_ADMIN by default.
- hasAuthority("ORDER_READ") checks the exact authority string.
- Use roles for broad job functions and authorities/permissions for
  finer-grained capabilities. Be consistent in how authorities are
  created and checked.
When to use: Use @PreAuthorize when access depends on roles,
permissions, ownership, method arguments, or a combination of
conditions.
2.4 @PostAuthorize
Purpose: Evaluates an authorization expression after the method
has executed. The returned object is available as returnObject.

Example:
@PreAuthorize("isAuthenticated()")
@PostAuthorize("returnObject.ownerUsername == authentication.name or hasRole('ADMIN')")
public Document getDocument(Long documentId) {
    return documentRepository.findById(documentId)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new DocumentNotFoundException(documentId));
}
This can be useful when the authorization decision depends on data that
is only known after retrieving the object.

Caution: The method has already run before the post-authorization
check. Do not rely on @PostAuthorize to protect operations that cause
side effects, such as updating records, transferring money, or sending
messages. Prefer checking permission before performing such operations.
For reads, consider whether loading the object before denying access is
acceptable.
2.5 @Secured
Purpose: Restricts method access to users with one of the specified
security roles/authorities. It is a simpler, role-oriented alternative
to @PreAuthorize.

Example:
@Secured({"ROLE_ADMIN", "ROLE_MANAGER"})
public void approveOrder(Long orderId) {
    // approval logic
}
Enable it using:
@EnableMethodSecurity(securedEnabled = true)
Limitations: @Secured does not support the rich SpEL expressions
available with @PreAuthorize. For new code requiring ownership or
argument-based rules, @PreAuthorize is usually a better fit.
2.6 @RolesAllowed
Purpose: JSR-250 annotation that allows a method to be invoked by
callers with one of the listed roles.

Example:
@RolesAllowed({"ADMIN", "MANAGER"})
public void updateEmployee(Employee employee) {
    // update logic
}
Enable JSR-250 support:
@EnableMethodSecurity(jsr250Enabled = true)
Role naming and prefix handling should be checked against the
application's granted authorities and Spring Security configuration. Do
not assume that every annotation family handles role names identically
without verifying the configured role prefix.
2.7 @PermitAll
Purpose: JSR-250 annotation that permits any caller to invoke the
annotated method, including an unauthenticated caller, subject to the
rest of the application's security configuration.

Example:
@PermitAll
public Product getPublicProduct(Long productId) {
    return productRepository.findById(productId)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new ProductNotFoundException(productId));
}
Enable with @EnableMethodSecurity(jsr250Enabled = true).

Caution: Use this deliberately. A method-level permit rule does not
mean that every surrounding URL, filter, network layer, or other
security control is bypassed.
2.8 @DenyAll
Purpose: JSR-250 annotation that denies invocation to every caller.

Example:
@DenyAll
public void legacyAdministrativeOperation() {
    // deliberately inaccessible
}
Enable with @EnableMethodSecurity(jsr250Enabled = true).

It can be useful as a deliberate deny rule, for example while disabling
a legacy operation. In most cases, removing an unused method or endpoint
is preferable to leaving dead code exposed.
2.9 @AuthenticationPrincipal
Purpose: Injects the current authenticated principal into a
controller method argument. It avoids manually reading the
SecurityContextHolder in ordinary controller code.

Example:
@GetMapping("/me")
public UserProfile getMyProfile(
        @AuthenticationPrincipal CustomUserPrincipal principal) {
    return userService.getProfile(principal.userId());
}
A typical custom principal might be:
public record CustomUserPrincipal(
        Long userId,
        String username
) implements UserDetails {
    // Implement the remaining UserDetails methods as required.
}
The actual principal type depends on the authentication mechanism. With
JWT resource-server authentication, for example, the principal may be a
Jwt or another configured representation. Match the argument type to
the principal your application actually uses.

Caution: Handle anonymous requests and nullable principals
appropriately if the endpoint permits anonymous access.
3. Complete example: request security plus method security
The following example shows both layers in a Spring Boot application. It
uses HTTP Basic only to keep the example small; a production API may use
OAuth 2.0 resource-server JWT authentication or another suitable
mechanism.
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableMethodSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/public/**").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());

        return http.build();
    }
}
Service:
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @PreAuthorize("hasAuthority('ORDER_READ')")
    public Order getOrder(Long orderId) {
        return orderRepository.findById(orderId)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new OrderNotFoundException(orderId));
    }

    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN') or @orderAuthorization.isOwner(#orderId, authentication)")
    public void cancelOrder(Long orderId) {
        // Validate the order state and perform the cancellation.
    }
}
The orderAuthorization bean represents application-specific ownership
logic. Keep complex authorization rules in a well-tested component
rather than writing very long SpEL expressions.
4. Request-level security vs method-level security
  Aspect                  Request-level security  Method-level security
  Configured through      SecurityFilterChain   @EnableMethodSecurity and method
annotations

  Protects                URL/request patterns    Spring-managed method invocations

  Example                 /admin/** requires    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
ADMIN

  Best for                Broad endpoint rules    Business rules, ownership,
fine-grained permissions
Reuse                   Usually tied to HTTP    Can protect service methods used by
request patterns        multiple entry points
Recommended approach: Use request-level rules as the first security
boundary and method-level rules to protect sensitive business
operations. Do not assume that hiding a button in the UI or securing
only a controller endpoint is sufficient.
5. Important implementation details a senior developer should know
5.1 Method security is proxy-based
Spring method security is commonly implemented using Spring AOP
proxies/interceptors. A call that goes through the Spring proxy can
trigger the authorization check.

A common pitfall is self-invocation:
@Service
public class ReportService {

    public void generate() {
        exportSensitiveReport(); // Direct call within the same object.
    }

    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public void exportSensitiveReport() {
        // ...
    }
}
The internal call from generate() to exportSensitiveReport()
normally does not pass through the Spring proxy, so the method-security
interceptor may not run for that call. Put the secured method on another
Spring bean or redesign the call so it crosses the proxy boundary. Avoid
relying on self-invocation for security enforcement.
5.2 Secure service-layer operations, not only controllers
A service method may be called by a REST controller, scheduled job,
message listener, or another service. Protecting important business
operations at the service layer helps keep authorization rules close to
the operation.

Still, choose the right boundary: scheduled jobs and message consumers
may need a deliberate service identity or authorization strategy rather
than assuming an HTTP user's context exists.
5.3 Use least privilege
Grant only the roles and permissions needed for a user or service to
perform its job. Avoid giving broad ADMIN access just to make an
integration work.
5.4 Return appropriate HTTP responses
For a typical HTTP API:
- 401 Unauthorized: authentication is missing or invalid (despite
  the historical wording, this means the caller is not successfully
  authenticated).
- 403 Forbidden: the caller is authenticated but is not allowed to
  perform the operation.
The exact response can be customized through authentication entry points
and access-denied handlers.
5.5 Do not confuse CORS and authorization
CORS controls which browser origins may make certain cross-origin
requests. It is not a replacement for authentication or authorization. A
non-browser client is not protected by CORS.
5.6 CSRF depends on the authentication and client model
For browser-based applications that authenticate using cookies or
sessions, CSRF protection is important. Stateless APIs using bearer
tokens in the Authorization header may have a different CSRF threat
model. Do not disable CSRF blindly; make the decision based on how
credentials are stored and sent.
5.7 Test security rules
Test both allowed and denied cases. Spring Security's test support
includes @WithMockUser and request post-processors such as user()
and jwt() (for relevant test setups).

Example:
@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
void adminCanReadAllUsers() {
    userService.getAllUsers();
}

@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
void normalUserCannotReadAllUsers() {
    assertThrows(AccessDeniedException.class,
        () -> userService.getAllUsers());
}
Depending on whether the test invokes a proxied service directly or
exercises the HTTP layer, configure the test context appropriately. For
MVC tests, use Spring Security's MockMvc integration and assert the HTTP
status as well.
